  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nAs the long term period begins on Sunday night, an upper level ridge \nover the south-central CONUS will be slowly building eastward. This \nridge and associated surface high pressure will amplify and set up \nover the east CONUS during the early parts of next week. Low level \nmoisture from the Gulf will persist across north and central \nGeorgia, though dewpoints are forecast to drop slightly into the \nupper 60s to low 70s with subsidence of drier air from the mid \nlevels under the ridge. This will also inhibit rain chances, with \nisolated afternoon thunderstorms being largely confined to south-\ncentral Georgia on Monday through Wednesday. Conditions across north \nand central Georgia are expected to be warmest on Monday, rising \ninto the mid 90s across all but the far northeastern mountains. Heat \nindex values are moreover forecast to climb to between 100-103 \ndegrees in areas south of I-85.\n\nThe axis of the ridge will begin to shift northeast of the forecast \narea on Tuesday into Wednesday, with the associated surface high \nmoving over the central Appalachians. As this occurs, it will spread \na CAD wedge into portions of northeast Georgia, leading to slightly \nlower dewpoints and high temperatures in the mid to upper 80s in the \nfar northeast and low 90s elsewhere across the forecast area. Rain \nchances will remain minimal on Tuesday and Wednesday under the \nridge, but will begin to rebound during the late parts of the week \nas the ridge begins to retrograde back to the west of the area.",
